{
  "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
  "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:06 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0000-0001",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nKEVIN K. RUSSELL, ESQ., Bethesda, Maryland; on behalf of\nPetitioner.\nSARAH E. HARRINGTON, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor\nGeneral, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on\nbehalf of the United States, as amicus curiae,\nsupporting Petitioner.\nJAMES C. HO, ESQ., Solicitor General, Austin, Texas; on\nbehalf of Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Kevin K. Russell)",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RUSSELL: By accepting Federal funds for\nits prisons, Texas consented to suit for appropriate\nrelief for violations of the Religious Land Use and\nInstitutionalized Persons Act. The question in this\ncase is whether that appropriate relief encompasses\ndamages."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Now, what would it be,\nMr. Russell, if there were a suit under RFRA because a\nFederal penal institution was not allowing for the\nreligious practices that the act protects?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Suppose Congress passes a\nstatute that creates a private right of action against\nboth the Federal Government and against the States and\nin both instances authorizes all appropriate relief.\nAre damages available in the action against the Federal\nGovernment as well as the State government?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Harrington",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the State voluntarily waives its immunity when it accepts Federal funds that clearly condition the acceptance of the funds on the waiver of its immunity. The State in this case doesn't contest that it has waived its sovereign immunity voluntarily to some universe of suits to enforce RLUIPA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF JAMES C. HO ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ho",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The phrase \"appropriate relief\" is a textbook example of ambiguity, not unmistakable clarity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What do you do with the practical problem that's been brought up that if a State is sued, it can release the prisoner, it can transfer the prisoner, and then no relief is appropriate? That the only way that the State is going to take its obligation seriously is if it's exposed to compensatory damages?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Ho",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. HO: If a prisoner is transferred, released, or the State simply changes its mind and gives up and provides the accommodation, in all those situations, the prisoner is no longer suffering from the complaint of condition. That's why this Court's mootness doctrines would apply."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: That's an ideal world, that they'll respond, but there is an allegation that some prisons wait until the eve of the trial after onerous discovery on the plaintiff and after enormous harm to plaintiffs, physical and otherwise, before they capitulate. So what's the inducement?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Let me ask -- get this information from you. As I understand it, there are some cases that find the words \"appropriate relief\" in a statute to include damages and there's some that don't. Let's look at the ones that don't."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: You -- you have already addressed this but I think it's their -- the position of your friend on the other side of the case is that with the Spending Clause you have a contract. The State has some extra protection, and therefore we need not be quite so strict in -- in construing waivers -- waivers of immunity, because you have the contract. Can you comment on that argument? And -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "319289d27b2a0a54aee18a0abff80cc3bd196d66f744fd59b3c04fd1183980c2",
      "evidence_id": "ev-319289d27b2a-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Was that contested in Barnes?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-08-1438",
      "confidence": 0.0,
